00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.829 align:start position:0%
 
i never saw it coming  end of an era  yeah  hey guys pete here what seems like years  ago now show runner peter gould promised  viewers that the final season a better  call saul would change the way we saw  breaking bad forever i never doubted him  for a minute because why would i this  team has pretty consistently earned my  trust but i also didn't expect how  thorough of a job they were setting out  to do a couple hours after finishing fun  and games my head is still spinning  while i connect the dots i didn't love  what happened in this episode but i did  love how it happened i loved the way it  was set up i loved watching the  characters go through it and i suppose  most importantly i love the absolute and  unconditional clean break can i pull out  a much maligned bravo vince here because  one of the other favorite responses in  relation to the show which would bring  you an equal amount of vitriol was to  ponder if any certain thing that he did  was the moment jimmy mcgill turned into  saul goodman and then they just did it  they paired it with one of the most  expert time jump edits i've ever seen  and then it was done hi there saul  goodman nice to meet you it's all fun  and games until someone gets hurt  the thing this show has done better than  anything that came before it is to  deliver the inevitable in a way that's  still thrilling to watch we know where  things are going and we'd rather craft  elaborate theories that would explain  how it might turn out differently rather  than accept what we're about to see we  know that jimmy and saul exist inside  the same person but don't want to see  him change we know kim has to leave him  for that to happen but would rather  she'd stay we believe mike is different  than his cohorts but don't see as much  of that in breaking bad and i could go  on they made us want things to be  different while never not telling us  exactly how they'd be this episode's  ending actually begins in the last one  when lalo and howard are buried together  under the future super lab there are  some obvious takeaways built into that  scene it's a visual representation of  the two sides of the story coming  together there's a lawyer laying next to  the cartel it's awful that howard ends  up that way buried next to his killer  nonetheless and you can't miss mike's  regret as he tells his guys to take it  easy with the body we know mike's  discomfort is about howard not being in  the game it doesn't fit with the code  that he's crafted to help make his  working with gus make sense the other  layer here is that no code can account  for the world of destruction that what  they do brings people make choices for  what seem like good reasons mike wants  to provide for his daughter-in-law and  his granddaughter because he can't  forgive himself for not being able to  protect his son he copes with what he  believes he has to do by trying to  dictate how far he'd go while he's  pursuing that but in a way he's just  lying to himself fast forward to him  trying to do the right thing by going to  visit manuel varga and tell him about  his son nacho someone who by the end  mike truly cared for when he tells him  that he fell in with some bad people but  was never liked them not really he had a  heart he's basically describing what he  thinks about himself papa varga is able  to deliver a truth that might conceive  but not accept there is no justice in  killing nacho's killers because it's  just about revenge it doesn't change  what happened and what's worse is that  it doesn't deliver resolution the next  thing leads to the next thing and it  never ends i believe it's likely that  the better part of mike understands this  and that means that in breaking bad he's  making a conscious decision to overlook  that it's a hell of a way to bring his  better call saul story to an end we know  where his story goes from here and this  moment with manuel will paint a lot of  his choices in a different light this is  something that i'll come back around to  but if we're talking about revenge we  might as well talk about what his boss  was up to in this episode  gus is summoned for a quick trip to  mexico where he crushes hector one more  time as balsa was reading his letter i  found myself wondering just how long it  would take for him to dictate that to  the cousins and to compare that to how  long eladio took to dismiss it in favor  of his top earner lalo did such a good  job of setting up his fake death that he  made his uncle look like a delusional  jealous crank that wasn't getting his  way hector isn't the type of character  that you feel bad for after all he  relished killing max in front of gus a  few feet away from where they are but  you do still feel something as the sound  of his ringing bell fades as he's  carried away gus defeated lalo and he  got away with it we see that start to  set in and we see him do something we've  never seen before he goes out to  celebrate he has a pleasant uplifting  conversation about fine wine that's  packed with sexual tension and it's a  complete reversal from the paranoia he's  been living with throughout this season  he has the money bottle of wine at home  that's just waiting to be shared and he  does allow himself to entertain that  fantasy only for a minute though before  his friend returns he pays up and leaves  and that felt like the conclusion of his  involvement in this prequel we know how  single-minded his purpose is we know  where that takes him and as much as this  show did to make him a little scarier a  little more sociopathic i feel like this  made his character a little more sad  he'll give up everything else to get  what he wants and he'll be successful in  making the cartel and the salamanca  family pay and just like this  potentially hot romance here he'll never  get to enjoy it in relation to their  plan against howard kim and jimmy also  got what they wanted they made him look  bad and they got away with it but their  victory came with a very real unintended  consequence that led to him being in  that hole at the end of episode 8. in  the season's first montage we watch as  they're more or less able to go back to  their lives in the wake of that disaster  as mike and his guys make their  apartment look like it never happened  after they come home they briefly move  into a hotel that happens to have the  same painting above the bed that walt  commented on when he first met uncle  jack and when jimmy parrots mike's  advice from last season that he got from  stacy and their group back in season 3  there's a sense that they may be able to  put the whole thing behind them it's not  until they return to hhm that we get to  take their temperatures and it turns out  they're ice cold on the way in jimmy  says they can get through 20 minutes of  anything and based on a reddit comment i  saw i went back and checked and if you  skip ahead 20 minutes from where he says  that you'll see saul in a silk robe  managing his comb over in the mirror  howard's memorial is a high stress  affair presumably everyone who knew and  loved him is there i feel like the actor  who played ernesto must not have been  available for this because this would  have been the perfect time to show that  he's moved on and is thriving after his  situation with chuck we do get a final  scene with rich schweikart and it's  funny in a way that they have all these  mounted pictures around that look like  they could be from patrick fabian's  personal instagram account things get  heavy when they go to pay their respects  to his wife cheryl she brings up that  her husband told her that jimmy was  harassing him which we saw howard do in  her brief appearance back in episode 6.  it's not a surprise that jimmy can  deflect the accusations while appearing  to be earnest and respectful he knocks  it out of the park just like you'd  expect still cheryl can't believe howard  was on drugs and starts to get upset  this creates a situation where kim has  to step up with a lie that goes above  and beyond she comes up with exactly  what's needed while at the same time  it's something you'd never want this  character to be involved with she  doesn't just make up a story that never  happened which ends up being  corroborated by cliff's inability to  answer which of course is related to  their scam but when cheryl starts to  believe it she leans in to pretend she's  on her side and well that's difficult to  watch and i think what's worse than the  aftermath of what their choices have led  to all of this that's happening in these  last couple of episodes what's worse is  that they're just so damn good at doing  this  as they go out to the parking garage we  get a glimpse of the spot where we first  saw them interact in that famous scene  in the first episode of the series and  then jimmy tries to put a positive spin  on things  then we get what i think is the best  race seahorn as kim seen in the entire  series i know recency bias but in this  shot where she doesn't say anything  where she just looks at her husband and  contemplates what he just said before  she moves closer to kiss him i knew that  it was over it takes a second to hit but  it feels impossible to miss not to be  outdone bob odenkirk does a fantastic  job of letting us see that jimmy knows  it too after she drives away even if  he's not ready to accept it because  we're not ready to either they give us a  courtroom scene to drive it home ever so  slowly by the time the judge has just  about had it with her not wanting to  move forward with her current case kim  admits that she's no longer a lawyer at  first i was confused about why she made  this step if you don't want to practice  fine but why make it official i think  this comes back to the idea of justice  that we heard papa varga bring up part  of this quest to take down howard was  born in the belief that kim could decide  who deserves it what's a career setback  for a powerful lawyer in comparison to  all the help that she could give to  underprivileged people and she wasn't  necessarily wrong all things considered  tipping the scales in the other  direction would amount to some good and  even howard realized that he would  bounce back before he died i think she  realized the part that manuel pointed  out to mike that it never ends and when  things go as wrong as they did and you  have to live with the consequences of  your choices you can't continue to tip  the scales to try to make up for it  jimmy races home after he gets the news  that she withdrew from the bar and i  liked how they showed us that her mind  was made up as she calmly watches him  pull into this parking spot and then  mostly just listens as he freaks out  it's hard to watch for us but his  reaction is to be expected when he  bursts into their room to see that she's  packing to leave she brings up him  asking if he was bad for her and if you  recall that scene that's where she  decided that she was gonna do something  to try to lift his spirits which  eventually sets things in motion for  going after howard she assures jimmy  that that's not the problem that the  truth is that they're bad for each other  and when he starts to buckle under the  weight she says jimmy i have had the  time of my life with you but we are bad  for everyone around us other people  suffer because of us apart we're okay  but together we're poison this is  followed by a wonderfully painful bit of  pleading that ends with jimmy finally  saying i love you for the first time on  screen and this is where you see her  break a little as she tears up and says  but so what and as devastating as this  is i think it shows how genuine their  feelings are for each other it doesn't  play like this is his first time ever  saying this out loud and her coming back  with us so what i think it drives home  the idea that they say it to each other  all the time off screen and that this  breakup isn't about them not feeling  that way anymore they went out of their  way to show just how real that was in  the last episode and this conversation  and her reasoning for the breakup all  reinforces that it's not tragic because  they're falling out of love it's that  way because they are in love and you  understand that they'll continue to feel  that way but they can't be together if  that wasn't enough when pushed about  what happened to howard not being her  fault kim confesses that she knew she  tells him that she kept this a secret  because she knew what he'd do and that  would lead to them breaking up which is  something she didn't want because she  was having too much fun on some level  jimmy understands this and realizes  there's nothing else he can say we just  watched his heart crumble from behind as  she goes to the room and finishes her  packing it's the scene i was joking  about in the beginning and after that  incredible jump to him waking up on a  rotating bed with a sex worker we know  his transformation is complete this  flash forward is more than a stinger it  fully immerses us in the breaking bad  timeline before the episode ends there's  a lot of interesting stuff going on but  the real point is that this is what his  life looks like behind the scenes it's  something that we never got to look at  in the original series and it works  really well to show how different he is  now that kim is no longer in his life it  makes sense when you look at everything  that happened and if you ever thought  during the series that you couldn't wait  to get saul back how do you feel about  it now i doubt that we've seen kim for  the last time there's the obvious chance  of her coming back during the gene  timeline and i think it's possible that  with four episodes to go that there  could be even some scenes to show what  her post-albuquerque life looks like for  saul we're crossing over into old  territory but it still feels like  anything could happen plus there's still  that call on his birthday that he  mentioned in the 405 teaser at this  point she still seems like the most  likely person to be calling which means  that they aren't completely out of  contact i thought they did a fantastic  job of hitting each point on the way to  this conclusion you hate to see it but  it was amazing to watch using mike and  gus to show where revenge and this  skewed sense of justice takes you and  then bringing it all back around to  kim's choices worked so much better than  you would have guessed it could as it  became clear that kim would probably not  be around past this episode i was at a  place where i didn't like any of the  options partly because i didn't want the  relationship to end but more because  most of the popular ones will require  something drastic to happen at best or  at worst have someone or something make  that decision for her i truly appreciate  that they decided to go with the idea  that a character can be more than one  thing at the same time i think that this  totally tracks with the kim we've gotten  to know even though she was so committed  to the scam she seemed proof that jimmy  would never be able to do it and i think  part of what's always been interesting  about these two is that despite being  unbelievably capable on their own they  still needed each other it makes it  tough to watch her make this decision  knowing that and if you can't be sure  where it leads or knowing what this  might do to this person that she cares  about at least you can say that she did  what she had to do by herself and i  think that's a good place to leave  things let me know in the comments what  you thought about the episode what you  think about the time jump and if we seen  the last of kim this is the type of  episode that the more i think about it  the more it reveals it just keeps going  so make sure you join us for the live  stream tuesday at 9 00 pm eastern time  because i'm sure there'll be a lot more  to say when we get there please like  this video if you enjoyed it please  subscribe to my channel if you haven't  already and thanks for watching i'll  talk to you soon

